1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:01,620
Can you hear a cup fill?

2
00:00:01,760 --> 00:00:03,720
Eyes closed,
just listening.

3
00:00:04,153 --> 00:00:06,043
Pour water into a cup,

4
00:00:06,048 --> 00:00:08,888
and the sound climbs
higher and higher.

5
00:00:09,293 --> 00:00:11,753
A computer can even
work out the water level

6
00:00:11,753 --> 00:00:13,193
from the sound alone.

7
00:00:13,987 --> 00:00:15,932
The empty space
above the water

8
00:00:15,932 --> 00:00:19,402
acts like a pipe,
closed at the water end.

9
00:00:19,402 --> 00:00:22,227
Sound bounces up
and down inside it.

10
00:00:22,620 --> 00:00:25,880
A short pipe rings higher
than a long one.

11
00:00:26,030 --> 00:00:28,505
A flute player changes
the note by changing

12
00:00:28,505 --> 00:00:30,230
the length of the pipe.

13
00:00:30,515 --> 00:00:32,040
As the water rises,

14
00:00:32,360 --> 00:00:35,550
the empty space shrinks,
and the pitch climbs.

15
00:00:36,420 --> 00:00:39,435
Less empty space,
a higher pitch.

16
00:00:39,750 --> 00:00:41,490
With 2 centimeters left,

17
00:00:41,490 --> 00:00:43,855
the cup rings
nearly three times higher

18
00:00:43,855 --> 00:00:44,990
than when empty.

19
00:00:45,820 --> 00:00:48,045
Measure it:
with 10 centimeters

20
00:00:48,045 --> 00:00:51,705
of empty space, you hear
about 700 vibrations

21
00:00:51,725 --> 00:00:52,825
a second.

22
00:00:52,980 --> 00:00:56,205
With 2 centimeters,
you hear nearly 2,000.

23
00:00:57,363 --> 00:00:59,923
A shorter pipe
makes a higher note.

24
00:01:00,063 --> 00:01:01,228
Next time you pour,

25
00:01:01,228 --> 00:01:03,648
listen: the cup
tells you its level.

26
00:01:04,320 --> 00:01:06,885
Next: a raindrop
falls a mile.

27
00:01:06,885 --> 00:01:07,737
Why doesn't it hurt?
